Now I have everything I need. Here's the full **testsmells.org 19-smell academic audit** for your integration test suite.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High | 3 | 3 |
| Medium | 1 | 1 |
| Low | 0 | 0 |
| **Total** | **4** | **4** |

**Suite:** MSTest 4.1.0 · .NET 10 · 1 file · 8 test methods
**Integration calibration applied:** class is tagged `[TestCategory("Integration")]` — Mystery Guest, Eager Test, and Sleepy Test severities adjusted per academic guidelines.

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic (Smell #1)

**Test:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 94–109)
**Taxonomy name:** *Conditional Test Logic*

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

The `if`/`else` means **one branch is never exercised**. If `loaded` is always a regular `User`, the `PremiumUser` path is dead code masquerading as a test. Neither developer nor CI will notice if the premium discount logic breaks.

**Fix — split into two explicit tests:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com")!;
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```
**Risk if unfixed:** Hidden test gap — one behaviour path is silently untested.

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test / Unknown Test (Smell #4)

**Test:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113–119)
**Taxonomy name:** *Unknown Test* (testsmells.org) / *Assertion-Free Test*

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertions
}
```

This test will **always pass** as long as no exception is thrown. Silent data corruption, partial inserts, or duplicate-key swallowing would go undetected.

**Fix — add meaningful assertions:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count, "All 100 bulk-inserted users should be persisted.");
}
```
**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — coverage reports show this code as tested when nothing is actually verified.

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test (Smell #3)

**Test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82–90)
**Taxonomy name:** *Sleepy Test*
**Integration calibration:** Noted as integration test. Severity remains **High** — `Thread.Sleep` is a smell even in integration tests per the academic catalog (delays cause non-deterministic failures and slow the suite).

```csharp
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    Thread.Sleep(3000);  // ← blocks thread for 3 seconds

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
}
```

**Additional concern:** This is an `async Task` method using **synchronous** `Thread.Sleep` instead of `await Task.Delay`, which blocks the thread pool.

**Fix — use a polling loop with timeout:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
    var interval = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100);
    var elapsed = TimeSpan.Zero;

    while (elapsed < timeout)
    {
        if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
            break;
        await Task.Delay(interval);
        elapsed += interval;
    }

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        "Notification should have been sent within 5 seconds.");
}
```
**Risk if unfixed:** 3-second fixed delay on every run (flaky if CI is slow, wasteful if notification is fast). Research (Camara et al., SAST 2021) correlates Sleepy Test with flaky test outcomes.

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette (Extended Smell)

**Test:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28–39)
**Taxonomy name:** *Assertion Roulette*

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);           // which failed?
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);   // which failed?
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email); // which failed?
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);       // which failed?
```

Four assertions, **zero message parameters**. When one fails in CI, the developer sees only `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<null>` with no context about which property check or why.

**Fix — add message parameters:**
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "Inserted user should be retrievable by email.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should survive round-trip persistence.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should survive round-trip persistence.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Persisted user should have a database-assigned positive ID.");
```
**Risk if unfixed:** Slower failure triage in CI — developers must read source to identify which property failed and why.

